¶
{{smaller block|The ancient ''avocation'' of picking pockets.
{{right|''Sydney Smith.''}}
In a few hours, above thirty thousand men left his standard, and returned to their ordinary ''avocations.''
{{right|''Macaulay'', Warren Hastings.}}
The wandering ''avocation'' of a shepherd.
{{right|''Buckle'', Civilization, II. i.}}}}

'''avocative†''' (a-vok′ạ-tiv), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [< ''avocate'' + ''-ive.'' Cf. ''vocative.''] '''I.''' ''a.'' Calling off. ''Smollett.'' '''II.''' ''n.'' That which calls aside; a dissuasive.
{{smaller block|Incentives to virtue, and ''avocatives'' from vice.
{{right|''Barrow'', The Creed.}}}}

'''avocato''' (av-ọ̄-kä′tō), ''n.'' Same as ''avocado.''

'''avocatory''' (a̤-vok′a̤-tọ̄-ri), ''a.'' [< ''avocate'' + ''-ory.''] Calling off; recalling.—'''Letters avocatory''', {{smaller|letters by which the subjects of a sovereign are recalled from a foreign state with which he is at war, or which bid them abstain or desist from illegal acts.}}

'''avocet,''' ''n.'' See ''avoset.''

'''Avocetta''' (av-ọ̄-set′ä̤), ''n.'' [NL.: see ''avoset.''] '''1.''' A genus of birds, the avosets: a synonym of ''Recurvirostra'' (which see). ''Brisson'', 1760. See cut under ''avoset.''—'''2.''' A genus of humming-birds. ''Agassiz.'' Also ''Avocettula.''

'''Avogadro’s law.''' See ''law.''

